@* A mailto that survives Cloudflare.

   Scrape Shield's Email Address Obfuscation is ON for these zones, and it rewrites every
   mailto in the HTML into "[email protected]" plus a JS decoder. On a shop's contact page that
   is actively harmful: with JavaScript off — a crawler, a bank or a credit agency checking
   that the business is reachable — the address is not hidden from spammers, it is simply
   gone. Cloudflare's documented opt-out is to bracket the section in <!--email_off--> ...
   <!--email_on-->.

   Those have to be written as MarkupString: the Razor compiler strips HTML comments from
   component output, so a literal <!--email_off--> in a .razor file never reaches the browser
   and the obfuscation happens anyway. This was found by grepping the live page, not the
   source — the source looked correct. *@

@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Address))
{
    @EmailOff
    <a href="mailto:@Address" class="@Class">@(Text ?? Address)</a>
    @EmailOn
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The address. Blank renders nothing at all — a shop may not have set one.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Address { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Link text. Defaults to the address itself.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Text { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    private static readonly MarkupString EmailOff = new("<!--email_off-->");
    private static readonly MarkupString EmailOn = new("<!--email_on-->");
}
